 "user_scenario": "You are Gunnar Lindqvist, 45, an HVAC service technician in Mobile, Alabama. You financed a 2019 Toyota Camry through Harborline and you have just moved your money to a new credit union; you want to pay the car off today, in full, over the phone, from your new checking account, so you are done with the loan. That is the only thing you are calling about. A couple of weeks ago you got a payoff figure from Harborline when you were about to sell the car, but the sale fell through.\n\nYou are direct and a little impatient, the way someone on a work break is, but polite. You have one account with Harborline. When the representative asks you to confirm who you are, you give your phone number, 2515550112, the last four of your Social Security number, 1573, and your date of birth, 1981-07-02; if they want your ZIP code it is 36606, and your account number is 7210593318 if they ask for it. If they ask about your October payment, you say you paid it by phone in the middle of the month from your old bank account and then closed that account when you switched banks, so it may have bounced. If they ask for the bank details for the payoff, you read them off your new checkbook: routing number 062203751, account number 4417209385.\n\nIf the representative explains that something has to be recorded on the account before they can take the payoff, you tell them to go ahead and do it. You want to pay by giving the representative your new checking account over the phone, not by mailing anything. When they tell you the payoff amount and hand the payment to you to authorize, you authorize it yourself for exactly the amount they give you. Once the representative confirms the payoff went through and the loan is paid off, you thank them and hang up."
